{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"crisis-response-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Crisis response track","description":"Runs communications through a live crisis - triage, first statement, stakeholders and media, updates to resolution, and an after-action review - pausing for approval at each step.","category":"public-relations","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","build","operate","review"],"role":["executive","marketer","founder","operations-manager"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","notes","message"],"output":["plan","copy","table","report"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["crisis-communications","incident-response","holding-statement","stakeholder-updates","after-action-review"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["write-holding-statement","write-public-apology-statement","reply-to-journalist-request","build-crisis-comms-plan"],"personas":["pr-strategist"]},"args":[{"name":"situation","description":"What is happening as far as you know, when it started, who is affected, what is already public, and what is being done.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"confirmed_facts","description":"Only the facts confirmed by the people responsible. Keep rumours and suspicions in the situation field.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"organisation","description":"Who you are, your sector and country, the crisis team you have, and any existing crisis plan. Optional.","type":"text"},{"name":"slug","description":"Short kebab-case name for the incident, used for the folder the step artifacts are saved in.","type":"string","default":"incident"}],"steps":[{"id":"triage","file":"steps/01-triage.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"crisis/{{slug}}/01-triage.md"},{"id":"first-statement","file":"steps/02-first-statement.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"crisis/{{slug}}/02-first-statement.md"},{"id":"stakeholders-and-media","file":"steps/03-stakeholders-and-media.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","artifact":"crisis/{{slug}}/03-stakeholders-and-media.md"},{"id":"updates","file":"steps/04-updates.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","artifact":"crisis/{{slug}}/04-updates.md"},{"id":"after-action","file":"steps/05-after-action.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","artifact":"crisis/{{slug}}/05-after-action.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Runs the communications side of a live crisis the way an experienced crisis adviser would: triage first, say something true quickly, tell the right people in the right order, keep updates on a promised rhythm until the matter is resolved, then learn from it. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval, because facts change fast and a human must decide what goes out. Later steps build on approved artifacts and on any new confirmed facts the user adds.\n\n<situation>\n{{situation}}\n</situation>\n\n<confirmed_facts>\n{{confirmed_facts}}\n</confirmed_facts>\n{{#organisation}}\n<organisation>\n{{organisation}}\n</organisation>\n{{/organisation}}\n\nRules for every step:\n- People's safety comes before reputation. If anyone may be in danger, the first line of every artifact says what they should do.\n- Only confirmed facts go into anything external. Keep a running list of what is confirmed, what is unconfirmed and who is checking it, and ask for updated confirmed facts at the start of each step.\n- No speculation about cause, scale or blame; no \"no comment\"; no reassurance the facts do not support.\n- Recommend legal review of external statements, and flag regulatory, insurer or authority notification duties for the user to confirm with advisers. Never state a legal deadline as fact.\n- Never name or describe affected individuals, and never draft anything meant to mislead, conceal or deny something that happened.","steps":[{"id":"triage","file":"steps/01-triage.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"crisis/{{slug}}/01-triage.md","text":"# Step 1: Triage\n\nWork out what kind of crisis this is and how fast the organisation must move.\n\n1. Safety first: is anyone at risk now? If so, write the safety instruction for affected people at the top.\n2. Facts board: a table of confirmed facts, unconfirmed claims (including what is circulating publicly), and open questions, each with who can confirm it.\n3. Severity: rate it (issue, serious, critical) by harm to people, number affected, public visibility, legal or regulatory exposure, and how fast it is spreading. Say what would raise or lower the rating.\n4. Clock: what is already public, where, and how fast it is moving; when the first statement must go out (usually within the hour for anything public).\n5. Team and decisions: who is on the crisis team, who approves statements, who is the spokesperson, and which advisers (legal, insurer, technical) to call now.\n6. Immediate holds: scheduled marketing, posts and ads to pause.\n\nStop and wait for approval and any new confirmed facts."},{"id":"first-statement","file":"steps/02-first-statement.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"crisis/{{slug}}/02-first-statement.md","text":"# Step 2: First statement\n\nSay something true quickly, using the approved triage.\n\n1. Holding statement for media and the public, about 80 to 150 words: what happened (confirmed only), care for those affected, what is being done, what affected people should do, and a committed time for the next update.\n2. Staff note that reaches staff before or with the public statement: what happened, what to say if asked (refer to the named contact), what not to post, when they will hear more.\n3. Short social version that links to the full statement, and a plan for replies: who monitors, which questions get a standard answer, which go to the crisis team.\n4. List the phrases deliberately left out and why, and the approvals needed before release.\n\nStop and wait for approval. Do not release anything without it."},{"id":"stakeholders-and-media","file":"steps/03-stakeholders-and-media.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","artifact":"crisis/{{slug}}/03-stakeholders-and-media.md","text":"# Step 3: Stakeholders and media\n\nTell the right people in the right order and prepare for questions.\n\n1. Stakeholder sequence: affected people, staff, board, regulators and authorities, insurers, key customers, partners and suppliers, investors, the local community. For each: order, who tells them, channel, and the message adapted from the approved statement. Mark notification duties to confirm with advisers.\n2. Reactive Q&A: the ten to fifteen hardest questions, including hostile ones, answered only from confirmed facts, with a bridge to actions and the next update.\n3. Media handling: how enquiries are logged and answered, deadlines tracked, the spokesperson's three key messages, and when to offer an interview versus a written statement.\n4. Monitoring: which channels and outlets to watch, what signals mean the story is growing, and the rumours to correct with facts.\n\nStop and wait for approval and new confirmed facts."},{"id":"updates","file":"steps/04-updates.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","artifact":"crisis/{{slug}}/04-updates.md","text":"# Step 4: Updates to resolution\n\nKeep the promised rhythm until the matter is resolved.\n\n1. Update the facts board with the new confirmed facts. Call out anything said earlier that is now wrong and draft the correction first.\n2. Draft the next update for each audience, at the committed time, saying what is new, what is still unknown and when the next update will come.\n3. When the cause and responsibility are established, decide with the user whether a full statement or an apology is needed, and draft it: specific acknowledgement, ownership without conditional phrasing, impact, remedy and changes with dates, from the accountable leader.\n4. Define what \"resolved\" means for communications (affected people helped, cause explained, changes committed) and draft the closing update.\n5. Plan when to resume normal marketing and how to handle the anniversary or follow-up coverage.\n\nStop and wait for approval. Repeat this step for each update cycle if the user asks."},{"id":"after-action","file":"steps/05-after-action.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","artifact":"crisis/{{slug}}/05-after-action.md","text":"# Step 5: After-action review\n\nLearn from the response while memories are fresh.\n\n1. Timeline of what happened and what was said, from the decision log and approved artifacts.\n2. What worked and what did not: speed of the first statement, approval delays, accuracy (anything that had to be corrected), stakeholder order, media handling, monitoring.\n3. Effect: coverage tone over time, questions and complaints volume, trust signals from customers or staff, using only data the user supplies.\n4. Changes: to the crisis plan, templates, decision rights, contact lists and training, each with an owner and date.\n5. Commitments made publicly during the crisis and whether each has been delivered.\n\nKeep the review blame-free: focus on systems and decisions, not individuals."}]}
